00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:09.280
or a package, whatever you want to call it.

550
00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:11.320
But really, at the end of the day, an agent skill

551
00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:14.400
and really just give thought to, okay, who are the audiences?

552
00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:16.240
What platforms are they operate on?

553
00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:20.600
Can I maintain the Readme here and sync to those registries or whatever it is?

554
00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:22.560
That should be kind of your objective.

555
00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:24.320
Add evaluations.

556
00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:24.880
That is key.

557
00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:26.960
If you don't do that, it's just going to fall apart.

558
00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.080
So make sure you define the bare minimum.

559
00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:31.760
What do I expect this skill to actually help people with?

560
00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:34.280
And how do I actually validate that? That's it.

561
00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:35.840
Just give a little thought to that up front.

562
00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:38.920
It'll go a long way and you can kind of compound on that over time.

563
00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.040
And then of course start sharing it.

564
00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:44.640
But you don't want to just share, you know, with everyone right away.

565
00:24:44.680 --> 00:24:46.800
You kind of use it yourself first.

566
00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:48.680
This is like the 0.0.

567
00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.040
x version of the context, right?

568
00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:53.800
You really do want to kind of semantically version your skills and your context

569
00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:56.200
to give signal to your engineers consuming it. Right?

570
00:24:56.200 --> 00:24:59.680
Because if I go look at a skill and it's version 1.0, I expect it to do

571
00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:02.760
exactly what it's supposed to do on the first try with almost no friction.

572
00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:03.120
Right?

573
00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:05.840
And if it doesn't do that, you've lost my trust, right?

574
00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.080
So signify that with your versioning.

575
00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:10.200
How mature is that actually right.

576
00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:13.600
So start with version zero zero, share it with a few, get some feedback.

577
00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:14.240
Iterate.

578
00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:17.560
Once it's becoming a little more stable, maybe zero dot x, right?

579
00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.240
And then finally, once you've shared it with a lot more people and it's regularly

580
00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:23.320
like people are like, man, this is working great for me.

581
00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:25.520
That's a good signal to move it to version one, right?

582
00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.120
And then that gives everyone else a signal, hey, you can consume this.

583
00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:29.080
Here's what the skill does.

584
00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:32.240
I promise it'll work because it's battle tested and evaluated.

585
00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:37.000
So takeaways here again our agent architectures they change every day.

586
00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:39.040
You'll have a new one next week. Next month.

587
00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:40.200
You really can't predict that.

588
00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.720
But your context is your durable investment right?

589
00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:47.520
Really spend time on how you contacts engineer and maintain things in one place,

590
00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:50.520
but distribute that to multiple systems to keep them in sync.

591
00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:52.200
That's really where you want to spend your time

592
00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:55.200
and just build your pipelines today, because everything else is definitely

593
00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:56.600
going to change on you anyway.

